## Documentation policy

Documentation is a live specification and part of every logical code commit.

When behavior, APIs, functions, architecture, configuration, schemas, migrations, or operations change:

- update the authoritative docs in the same commit;
- explain implementation, function, purpose, intent, and protected goals/invariants;
- document contracts, errors, side effects/state, and relevant failure behavior;
- update examples, ADRs, migration/rollback, and runbooks as applicable;
- run repository-native documentation checks.

Do not add comments that merely restate obvious code.

## Agent workflow

Use `Dev` / `/dev` for development.

Use `ReviewPR` / `/review-pr` for another developer's PR.

Decompose non-trivial work into coherent, independently committable units with explicit dependencies and ownership. Every unit follows `plan -> implement -> document -> simplify -> verify`. Parallel writers use isolated worktrees. Do not claim completion without executable, complexity, and documentation evidence. After the committed-range gate passes, invoke `eli5` and include the checked visual handoff path.
